    {
      "id": 17592186048774,
      "mathAcademyId": 375,
      "title": "Solving Quadratic Equations by Factoring",
      "uuid": "bd7f4972-3745-5dac-a650-fd77c0edd057"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186111830,
      "id": "6e6723bc-33e2-5ea4-a243-26c8cdf1e200",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-3x+1=0\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to factor the trinomial on the left-hand side. We'll need to find two numbers such that\n\n- their product is equal to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2 \\times 1 = 2$), and\n- their sum is equal to the linear coefficient (which is $-3$).\n\nThese two numbers are $-1$ and $-2$. So, we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 3x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2}\\underbrace{-1x - 2x}_{-3x} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(2x^{2} - x) + (-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(2x - 1) - 1(2x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2x-1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},1\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "1626",
      "stepId": "17592186111698",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186110329,
      "id": "5dcdeb28-45af-5177-9667-a170e74144be",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186110729,
        "title": "Solving When the Terms Have No Common Factor"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve the equation $-2x^2 +9x -4=0$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1424",
      "stepId": "17592186111699",
      "title": "Solving When the Terms Have No Common Factor",
      "workedSolution": "First, we multiply by $-1$ so that the leading coefficient becomes positive.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x^{2} + 9x - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-2x^{2} + 9x - 4) &= (-1) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 9x + 4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nand add to $-9$. These two numbers are $-1$ and $-8$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x^2 -9x +4 &=0 \\\\ 2x^2 -1x - 8x +4 &=0 \\\\ (2x^2 -1x) + (-8x +4) &= 0 \\\\ x(2x-1) -4(2x-1) &= 0 \\\\ (2x-1)(x-4) &=0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2x-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x-4=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},4\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186110330,
      "id": "815e72b5-e368-578c-b3b9-7ddfa02e23f5",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186110730,
        "title": "Solving When the Terms Have a Common Factor"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve the equation $45x^2 +24 x -36 =0$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1425",
      "stepId": "17592186111700",
      "title": "Solving When the Terms Have a Common Factor",
      "workedSolution": "First, notice that $45$, $24$ and $36$ have a common factor of $3$. So, we can divide by $3$ to simplify our equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{45x^{2} + 24x - 36}{3} &= \\frac{0}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{45}{3}x^{2} + \\frac{24}{3}x - \\frac{36}{3} &= \\frac{0}{3} \\\\\n15x^{2} + 8x - 12 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n15 \\cdot (-12)=-180\n$$\n\nand sum to $8$. These two numbers are $-10$ and $18$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 15x^2 +8 x -12 &=0 \\\\ 15x^2 -10x +18 x -12 &=0 \\\\ (15x^2 -10x) + (18 x -12) &= 0 \\\\ 5x(3x-2) + 6(3x-2) &= 0 \\\\ (5x+6)(3x-2) &=0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n5x+6=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 3x-2=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{6}{5} \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{6}{5}, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186110333,
      "id": "a94a8901-e3e7-57d4-a753-d384326a88c1",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186110731,
        "title": "Solving When the Right-Hand Side is Not Zero"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve the equation $7x^2-x+1=6x-3x^2$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1426",
      "stepId": "17592186111701",
      "title": "Solving When the Right-Hand Side is Not Zero",
      "workedSolution": "First, we bring all terms to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7x^2-x+1&=6x-3x^2 \\\\ 7x^2-x+1 -6x+3x^2&=0 \\\\ (7x^2+3x^2) + (-x-6x) +1 &=0 \\\\ 10x^2-7x+1 &=0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n10 \\cdot 1=10\n$$\n\nand sum to $-7$. These two numbers are $-2$ and $-5$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 10x^2-7x+1 &= 0 \\\\ 10x^2-2x-5x+1 &= 0 \\\\ (10x^2-2x) + (-5x +1) &= 0 \\\\ 2x(5x -1) - (5x -1) &= 0 \\\\ (2x-1)(5x-1)&=0 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2x-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 5x-1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$"
    }
